Council</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="pmed-0040259-b009"><citation citation-type="other"><collab>Department of Health, Medical Research Council &#x00026; Measure DHS&#x0002b;</collab><year>2002</year><article-title>South Africa Demographic and Health Survey</article-title><comment>Preliminary report. Pretoria: Department of Health. Available: <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.doh.gov.za/facts/index.html">http://www.doh.gov.za/facts/index.html</ext-link>. Accessed 25 July 2007</comment></citation></ref><ref id="pmed-0040259-b010"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group><name><surname>Solarsh</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Goga</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Child health</article-title><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Ijumba</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Day</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Ntuli</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><comment>editors</comment><source>South African health review</source><publisher-loc>Durban (South Africa)</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Health Systems Trust</publisher-name><fpage>101</fpage><lpage>128</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pmed-0040259-b011"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group><name><surname>Rudan</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>El Arifeen</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Black</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>A systematic methodology for setting priorities in child health research investments</article-title><source>Child Health and Nutrition Research Initiative(CHNRI): A new approach for systematic priority setting</source><publisher-loc>Dhaka (Bangladesh)</publisher-loc><publisher-name>CHNRI</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="pmed-0040259-b012"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group><name><surname>Costello</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Filippi</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><name><surname>Kubba</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Horton</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><year>2007</year><article-title>Research challenges to improve maternal and child survival</article-title><source>Lancet</source><volume>369</volume><fpage>1240</fpage><lpage>1243</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17434384</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pmed-0040259-b013"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group><collab>Working Group on Women and Child Health</collab><name><surname>Dabis</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Orne-Gliemann</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Perez</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>Improving child health: The role of research</article-title><source>Br Med J</source><volume>324</volume><fpage>1444</fpage><lpage>1447</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12065272</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pmed-0040259-b014"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group><name><surname>Leroy</surname><given-names>JL</given-names></name><name><surname>Habicht</surname><given-names>JP</given-names></name><name><surname>Pelto</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Bertozzi</surname><given-names>SM</given-names></name></person-group><year>2007</year><article-title>Current priorities in health research funding and lack of impact on the number of child deaths per year</article-title><source>Am J Pub Health</source><volume>97</volume><fpage>219</fpage><lpage>223</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17194855</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pmed-0040259-b015"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group><name><surname>Swingler</surname><given-names>GH</given-names></name><name><surname>Irlam</surname><given-names>JH</given-names></name><name><surname>Macharia</surname><given-names>WH</given-names></name><name><surname>Tietche</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Meremikwu</surname><given-names>MM</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>A systematic review of existing national priorities for child health research in sub-Saharan Africa</article-title><source>Health Res Policy Syst</source><volume>2005</volume><fpage>3</fpage><lpage>7</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pmed-0040259-b016"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group><name><surname>Sanders</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Chopra</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Key challenges to achieving health for all in an inequitable society: The case of South Africa</article-title><source>Am J Public Health</source><volume>96</volume><fpage>73</fpage><lpage>78</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16317201</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn id="fn1" fn-type="current-aff"><p>Mark Tomlinson is a senior specialist scientist with the Health Systems Research Unit, Medical Research Council, Cape Town, Western Cape, South Africa, and associate professor extraordinaire with the Department of Psychology, Stellenbosch University, Cape Town, Western Cape, South Africa. Mickey Chopra is Director of the Health Systems Research Unit, Medical Research Council, Cape Town, Western Cape, South Africa. David Sanders is Director of the School of Public Health, University of Western Cape, Cape Town, Western Cape, South Africa. Debbie Bradshaw is Director of the Burden of Disease Research Unit, Medical Research Council, Cape Town, Western Cape, South Africa. Michael Hendricks is an associate professor at the School of Child and Adolescent Health, University of Cape Town, Cape Town, Western Cape, South Africa. David Greenfield is a neonatologist at the Groote Schuur Hospital, University of Cape Town, Cape Town, Western Cape, South Africa. Robert E. Black is Professor and Chair of the Department of International Health, Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health, Baltimore, Maryland, United States of America. Shams El Arifeen is an epidemiologist and head of the Centre for Health and Population Research, Dhaka, Bangladesh. Igor Rudan is a senior research fellow with the Department of Public Health Sciences, The University of Edinburgh Medical School, Scotland, United Kingdom, and professor at the University of Split Medical School, Split, Croatia.</p></fn><fn id="ack1" fn-type="con"><p><bold>Author contributions.</bold> MT, MC, and IR had a major role in writing the paper. DS, SEA, MH, and DG had a role in writing the paper. DS, DB, MH, and DG participated in the conduct and interpretation of the South African case study. REB and SEA checked the paper for important intellectual content. MT, MC, DS, MH, DG, REB, SEA, and IR approved the final version of the paper.</p></fn><fn id="n101" fn-type="financial-disclosure"><p><bold>Funding:</bold> Child Health and Nutrition Research Initiative (CHNRI), a nonprofit effort of the Global Forum for Health Research, covered the expenses of several workshops and conferences at which the methodology was developed and provided support to the consultants named above for their assistance in the development of the methodology and its application within the area of their expertise. No other sources of funding were available for this work. The funder played no part in the decision to submit the article or in its preparation.</p></fn><fn id="n102" fn-type="conflict"><p><bold>Competing Interests:</bold> MT and MC have acted as consultants to the CHNRI to assist in its development and to apply this new methodology to priority setting at the national level (South Africa). REB is chairman of the board of the CHNRI, which developed the priority-setting methods described here. SEA is secretary of CHNRI. IR has been a consultant to the CHNRI, and has been leading the development of the new methodology to priority setting in child health and nutrition research. DS, DB, MH, and DG have no competing interests to report.</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>